		  How to interact with magma -x  
		  =============================

		   Geoff Bailey, December 2024

When invoked with the -x option, Magma switches to a mode where it
expects that another program is interacting with it.  As a result,
the way it handles output is different in order to allow the front
end to distinguish between the various states that it could be in.

There is a slight difference in behaviour if the standard input is
a terminal (prompts are printed, for a start).  I will ignore this
as the whole point of the -x option is to have Magma be interacted
with via a pipe or similar.


Simplified model
----------------

A simplified way of thinking about Magma in -x mode is that it can
be in one of two states: Waiting for input ("ready"), or executing
input ("running").  When it receives user input it goes from ready
to running; while running it may produce output; and once all user
input has been executed it goes back to being ready again.

Magma actually starts out in the running state, where it does such
things as printing the banner, loading any startup files, etc.


Input
-----

Input is no longer handled on a line-by-line basis; instead, input
is accumulated until a designated "end of input" character is sent
to Magma.  There are two options for this -- the character's value
can be either 4 (ASCII EOT) or 127 (ASCII DEL).  I do not know why
there are two options; my personal recommendation would be to just
use 4 (aka ctrl-D) for this purpose throughout.

End of input is also detected when closing Magma's input (the pipe
that connects to Magma), of course.  Magma will exit when it tries
to read more input and fails, but will first process any remaining
input.

This is arguably the more natural way to do things with a web-like
front end, Jupyter notebook, or similar, where the input resembles
an editor -- the user writes the desired commands (or modifies the
previous set) and then runs the entire collection once satisfied.

(It also avoids complications which might otherwise arise with the
read and readi directives.)

The input must consist of complete Magma statements.  If not, then
an error will result (see the ENE tag for details).  Typical usage
will not run into this issue.

The term "input set" will be used to refer to such a collection of
input.


Output
------

When Magma is in the running state it emits "tagged" output.  This
may provide information about the state of Magma, or could contain
normal output from the code being executed.  Tagged output is done
on a line basis; each line of output will begin with a special tag
marker, followed by a tag, then tag-specific data, and finished by
a newline.

The tag marker is a byte with value 129; this is unused in most of
the extended ASCII code pages and not a valid initial byte for the
UTF-8 coding, so it's not a terrible choice.  My inclination would
have been to use SOH and STX, though.

Important: Since the tag marker value is larger than 128, care may
be needed when checking it in platforms/languages which default to
signed characters.  (This is another reason to prefer SOH/STX.)

The tag will be a short string of uppercase letters; in some cases
an underscore may be part of the tag.  The tag will be followed by
either a space (byte value 32) or newline (byte value 10).  In the
former case it will be followed by further tag-specific data.

Syntax:

    <marker><tag><tag-data><newline>

where

  + <marker> is a byte with value 129.
  + <tag> is the text giving the name of the tag.  It will consist
    only of bytes with values between 65 and 90 (inclusive) (ASCII
    uppercase letters) or with value 95 (ASCII underscore).
  + <tag-data> is a (possibly empty) sequence of bytes that do not
    contain any byte with value 10 (ASCII newline character).  The
    format and meaning of this data depends on the specific tag.
  + <newline> is a byte with value 10 (ASCII newline character).

In the descriptions below, the <marker> and <newline> entries will
not be mentioned; we are concerned only with the tag and its data.


A note about indentation
------------------------

Magma frequently indents output to make it more readable.  This is
done using spaces in the terminal, but the front end might display
indentation in another way.  For instance, a web-based version may
use spacer spans of a fixed width, or left-margin hackery.

Magma lets the user set the number of spaces used for indentation;
the default value is four spaces.  If the front end is not a plain
terminal then it is encouraged to ignore the user's setting within
Magma and display indentations however it thinks best.  This might
involve a setting in the front end for such behaviour instead.

(Also, as mentioned later, getting this value at the right time is
slightly problematic even if it were desirable.)

Output tags (described later) may specify an indent level; this is
to be interpreted as the count of indentations to apply before the
text associated with that output tag is displayed.  In the case of
a terminal, then, an indent level of k would print 4*k spaces.

If SetColumns(0) has been done then Magma performs no indentation.
(I don't know why; it seems an odd decision to me.)  Consequently,
it is recommended not to do this.  Magma's behaviour in -x mode is
already special and it does not do line wrapping or splitting, and
thus this should not be necessary.

(By "does not do line splitting", I mean "does not break text with
a backslash-newline pair".  Long lines may produce multiple output
statements, but without introducing extra characters.  The section
on output tags explains this.)


Classes of tags
---------------

There are three classes of tags: Output tags, status tags, and the
others.  Each class shares a common format, although the last kind
is a bit loose on that front.  The formats will be described first
and then the specific tags will be covered later.


* Output tags.  Several tags are classed as "output tags" and have
  an identical format aside from the tag name.  The data is either
  the indent level to print the output at, or a special flag which
  indicates that this is a "continuation line".  Then a second tag
  marker is used, followed by text.  The given text is intended to
  be displayed to the user.

  Note that the text cannot contain a newline since a newline ends
  the tag data.  Indeed, the next output text might be intended to
  follow directly on from it without an intervening newline.  This
  is controlled by the continuation flag on that next output line,
  as described below.

  Consequently, the last output line is generally incomplete until
  the next tag causes output.  If a tag causes output, and expects
  that output to start a line, then it may need to print a newline
  before its output.  So it is necessary to track whether there is
  such a partially-printed line.  Also note that some tags may not
  cause output besides this newline; RDY is one such instance (see
  the description for that tag).

  The specific output tag used provides semantic information which
  the front end may use to change how it displays the output.  The
  description for such tags will explain this.

  Syntax:

	<tag><space><indent-level><marker><text>
	<tag><space><continuation-flag><marker><text>

  where

  + <tag> is the text giving the name of the tag.  It will consist
    only of bytes with values between 65 and 90 (inclusive) (ASCII
    uppercase letters) or with value 95 (ASCII underscore).
  + <space> is a byte with value 32 (ASCII space character).
  + <indent-level> is the usual ASCII printing of an integer.  The
    integer will not be negative and is expected to be small.
  + <marker> is a byte with value 129.
  + <text> is a (possibly empty) sequence of bytes.  At the moment
    it is limited to having at most 1024 bytes.
  + <continuation-flag> is a byte with value 67 (ASCII 'C').

  Behaviour:

  There may be some tag-specific behaviour to invoke (described in
  the section for that tag).  After that, the given text should be
  displayed.

  If the continuation flag was given, the text should be displayed
  immediately after the previous line of output (if any) from this
  input set, without any intervening newline.  If there is no such
  previous line within this input set, then display the text as if
  the indent level were given with value 0.

  If the indent level is given then this output starts a new line;
  if there is a previous line of output for this input set then do
  the equivalent of emitting a newline for your display.  Then, if
  the indent level is positive, display the text after that number
  of indentations.  If using terminal output, the default printing
  for one indentation is four spaces.

  Note: The user can change the number of spaces in an indentation
  with the SetIndent() intrinsic.  Unfortunately, there is no good
  way to use this.  The detailed description of the RDY tag says a
  little about this situation.


* Status tags.  Several tags are classed as "status tags" and have
  an identical format aside from the tag name.  In fact, that name
  is the entirety of the data for the tag.

  Loosely speaking, such tags indicate a change in the interpreter
  state; they may be accompanied by other tags which provide extra
  information.

  Syntax:

	<tag>

  where

  + <tag> is the text giving the name of the tag.  It will consist
    only of bytes with values between 65 and 90 (inclusive) (ASCII
    uppercase letters) or with value 95 (ASCII underscore).

  Behaviour:

  Some status tags are informational only; others may require that
  the front end act in a certain way.  The specifics will be given
  in the section for each tag.


* Data tags.  The remaining four tags (RDY, RUN, ERP, and POS) are
  classed as data tags.  They are similar to status tags, but come
  with information that can be used to provide extra functionality
  in the front end.

  Syntax:

	<tag>[<space><integer>]*

  where

  + <tag> is the text giving the name of the tag.  It will consist
    only of bytes with values between 65 and 90 (inclusive) (ASCII
    uppercase letters) or with value 95 (ASCII underscore).
  + <space> is a byte with value 32 (ASCII space character).
  + <integer> is the usual ASCII printing of an integer; this will
    never be negative, and will always fit within 64 bits.

  and '[', ']', and '*' have their usual meaning when in a regular
  expression context.  i.e., the above indicates that the tag will
  be followed by some number of instances of <space> and <integer>
  (the specific number is determined by the tag).

  Behaviour:

  These tags usually indicate a status change, but come with extra
  information which may be useful.  The specifics for each tag are
  in the appropriate section, of course.


Tags (short version)
--------------------

Here's the possible tags, in rough order of necessity to implement
in order to develop a working interface.  The meaning of the extra
information in data tags will be skipped here and explained in the
more detailed section later.

In the following, it is implicitly assumed that each input set has
its own output section.  This is very likely the case; even if the
terminal is being used, the input splits the output into sections.


* OUT is used for regular output from Magma.  It is an output tag.
  No special behaviour is invoked prior to printing the text.

  Syntax:

	OUT<space><indent-level><marker><text>
	OUT<space><continuation-flag><marker><text>

  Meanings and behaviour are as described for output tags above.

  Semantics:

  There are no special semantics for this tag.


* RDY indicates that Magma has finished with the current input set
  and is awaiting another one.  It is a data tag with five integer
  arguments.  The first four are either 0 or 1, while the fifth is
  non-negative and expected to be small in practice.

  The meaning of these integers is covered in the detailed section
  later.

  Syntax:

	RDY[<space><flag>]{4}<space><integer>

  where

  + <space> is a byte with value 32 (ASCII space character).
  + <flag> is a byte with value 48 (ASCII 0) or 49 (ASCII 1).
  + <integer> is the usual ASCII printing of an integer.

  and the '[..]{4}' notation indicates four instances of the items
  inside the square brackets.

  Behaviour:

  The calling program should seek another input set from the user,
  possibly after printing a prompt.

  Note: If the user was using the magma debugger (see the DRDY tag
  for more information), the presence of a RDY tag means that they
  have exited the debugger and the front end should handle that as
  appropriate.


* IR is a status tag, which indicates that input has been received
  and is about to be acted on.  It should only appear as the first
  line of response to an input set.

  Syntax:

	IR

  Behaviour:

  No behaviour is called for.  This tag seems entirely superfluous
  to me.


* RUN indicates that Magma is about to run the next statement from
  the input set.  It is a data tag with six integer arguments; all
  are non-negative.  The second argument potentially uses 64 bits,
  while the others should be at most 32-bits in size.

  The meaning of these integers is covered in the detailed section
  later.

  Syntax:

	RUN[<space><integer>]{6}

  where

  + <space> is a byte with value 32 (ASCII space character).
  + <integer> is the usual ASCII printing of an integer.

  and the '[..]{6}' notation denotes six instances of the items in
  the square brackets.


* QUIT is a status tag.  It will be printed as the last thing that
  Magma does before exiting normally.  It will usually be preceded
  by an output line giving the time and memory usage statistics.

  Syntax:

	QUIT

  Behaviour:

  The calling program should release any resources associated with
  the underlying Magma process and its connection to that.  It may
  need to start a new Magma process if there are more inputs to be
  dealt with.

  If the front end wishes to display the ending statistics in some
  alternative fashion, it will need to track the output and handle
  the line *before* QUIT specially.  This can be fiddly, but it is
  manageable.


* TB is an output tag, used while printing the traceback component
  of an error.

  Syntax:

	TB<space><indent-level><marker><text>
	TB<space><continuation-flag><marker><text>

  Meanings are as described for output tags above.

  Behaviour:

  The given text should be shown to the user.  However, there is a
  small problem because the indentation has been lost owing to how
  Magma manages printing of error objects.  The front end may wish
  to heuristically insert indentation so as to present this nicely
  to the user.

  A rule of thumb that handles most cases: If the text ends in '('
  or starts with ')' then it should not be indented; otherwise one
  level of indentation is to be applied.  This is not always right
  in principle, but in practice it will be almost all of the time.

  (The troublesome case: The function or intrinsic has a name that
  is so long it needs a continuation line, and thus that line does
  not end in a '(' and we erroneously indent it.  Correct handling
  involves tracking the traceback state to know when we expect the
  first line of a function/intrinsic.  It's not hard, but very low
  value.)

  Semantics:

  TB indicates that this output is part of a traceback.  This will
  only occur if an error has arisen; other error tags are expected
  to follow.


* EPO is an output tag.  It is used when printing output about the
  position of the error.  However, it does this unhelpfully, since
  it does not provide the position information in a defined format
  but instead as part of the message that Magma prints.  Also, the
  "pointer" that Magma usually prints pointing at the start of the
  error is omitted.

  Syntax:

	EPO<space><indent-level><marker><text>
	EPO<space><continuation-flag><marker><text>

  Meanings are as described for output tags above.

  Behaviour:

  The given text should be shown to the user.  The front end might
  choose to modify the appearance (e.g., colour) to conform to its
  policy for displaying errors, if it has one.

  Unfortunately, this only provides a human-readable message about
  the error position, and does not provide the position separately
  from that.  Also, the "pointer" that Magma usually gives showing
  the specific location of the error within the line is missing.

  An example is probably instructive here.  Eliding various parts,
  here's what the error might look like in normal magma, with 

	[traceback skipped]
	In file "/tmp/foo.m", line 18, column 13:
	>>     return 1/x;
	               ^
	Runtime error in '/': Division by zero

  And here is the corresponding section of output from magma -x:

	[traceback skipped]
	?EPO 0In file "/tmp/foo.m", line 18, column 13:
	?EPO 0>>     return 1/x;
	?ER 0Runtime error in '/': Division by zero
	?ER 0

  (The ? denotes the tag marker with value 129.)  The ER indicates
  a runtime error (covered below).  Note that we get the two lines
  of human-readable information, but no "pointer" line, nor is the
  position (column 13) given in a separate tag.

  To provide the in-line location information, the front end needs
  to parse it out from the first EPO message.  This is not good; I
  am looking into changing this, but since older magmas need to be
  supported anyway then this still needs to be handled.

  Semantics:

  EPO indicates that this output provides location information for
  an error.  This will only occur if an error has arisen, and will
  be followed by other error-related tags.


* ER is an output tag.  It indicates that the message relates to a
  runtime error.

  Syntax:

	ER<space><indent-level><marker><text>
	ER<space><continuation-flag><marker><text>

  Meanings are as described for output tags above.

  Behaviour:

  The given text should be shown to the user.  The front end might
  choose to modify the appearance (e.g., colour) to conform to its
  policy for displaying errors, if it has one.

  Note: Any error stops processing of the current input set.  Thus
  the next non-ER tag would normally be RDY (DRDY if debugging has
  been enabled).  However, if the interpreter has been set to quit
  on errors, the next non-ER tag will be QUIT.  The statistics for
  the time and memory usage that are printed at the end of a Magma
  session will have been delivered using ER instead of OUT.

  (I regard that as incorrect behaviour, but have not checked what
  would be involved in fixing that yet.)

  Semantics:

  ER indicates that this output describes the type of error, which
  is of the class of runtime errors.  There may be several ER tags
  in a row (usually at least two).


* ERP indicates that an error has occurred while parsing the input
  set.  It is a data tag with four integer arguments which are all
  non-negative.

  The meaning of these integers is covered in the detailed section
  later.

  Syntax:

	ERP[<space><integer>]{4}

  where

  + <space> is a byte with value 32 (ASCII space character).
  + <integer> is the usual ASCII printing of an integer.

  and the '[..]{4}' notation indicates four instances of the items
  inside the square brackets.

  Behaviour:

  The tag data contains position information that could be used to
  identify the malformed statement to the user.  It can be ignored
  at the cost of baffling the user.  (A textual description of the
  error will be printed later using the EU tag.)  See the detailed
  section later for how to interpret and use the tag data.


* POS indicates a position within the input set.  It is a data tag
  with two integer arguments which are all non-negative.

  The meaning of these integers is covered in the detailed section
  later.

  Syntax:

	POS[<space><integer>]{2}

  where

  + <space> is a byte with value 32 (ASCII space character).
  + <integer> is the usual ASCII printing of an integer.

  and the '[..]{2}' notation denotes two instances of the items in
  the square brackets.

  Behaviour:

  The tag data contains position information that could be used to
  identify the malformed statement to the user.  It can be ignored
  at the cost of baffling the user.  (A textual description of the
  error will be printed later using the EU tag.)  See the detailed
  section later for how to interpret and use the tag data.


* EU is an output tag.  It indicates that the message relates to a
  user error, which in practice means a parsing error.

  Syntax:

	EU<space><indent-level><marker><text>
	EU<space><continuation-flag><marker><text>

  Meanings are as described for output tags above.

  Behaviour:

  The given text should be shown to the user.  The front end might
  choose to modify the appearance (e.g., colour) to conform to its
  policy for displaying errors, if it has one.

  Note: Any error stops processing of the current input set.  Thus
  the next non-EU tag would normally be RDY (DRDY if debugging has
  been enabled).  However, if the interpreter has been set to quit
  on errors, the next non-EU tag will be QUIT.  The statistics for
  the time and memory usage that are printed at the end of a Magma
  session will have been delivered using EU instead of OUT.

  (I regard that as incorrect behaviour, but have not checked what
  would be involved in fixing that yet.)

  Semantics:

  EU indicates that this output describes the type of error, which
  is of the class of user errors.  Typically there is just the one 
  such tag, but not always (as per the behaviour section above).


* EI is an output tag.  It indicates that the message was produced
  by an internal error.

  Syntax:

	EI<space><indent-level><marker><text>
	EI<space><continuation-flag><marker><text>

  Meanings are as described for output tags above.

  Behaviour:

  The given text should be shown to the user.  The front end might
  choose to modify the appearance (e.g., colour) to conform to its
  policy for displaying errors, if it has one.

  Note: Any error stops processing of the current input set.  Thus
  the next non-EI tag would normally be RDY (DRDY if debugging has
  been enabled).  However, if the interpreter has been set to quit
  on errors, the next non-EI tag will be QUIT.  The statistics for
  the time and memory usage that are printed at the end of a Magma
  session will have been delivered using EI instead of OUT.

  (I regard that as incorrect behaviour, but have not checked what
  would be involved in fixing that yet.)

  Semantics:

  EI indicates that this output describes the type of error, which
  is of the class of internal errors.  There will probably be many
  EI lines for a single error.


* LST is an output tag.  It is used for data that forms a list and
  which Magma would usually format into columns.

  Syntax:

	LST<space><indent-level><marker><text>
	LST<space><continuation-flag><marker><text>

  Meanings are as described for output tags above.

  Behaviour:

  The given text should be shown to the user.  However, since this
  is only used for (possibly long) lists of identifiers, the front
  end might choose to accumulate all LST outputs first and display
  them in another way.  For instance, printed in columns or making
  a collapsible / expandable section for them.

  Semantics:

  LST output is used only for a few special cases corresponding to
  the outputs of the following intrinsics:

  + ListCategories / ListTypes
  + ListAttributes
  + ShowIdentifiers

  as well as the output of the "id" / "identifiers" command in the
  debugger.

  Note that these are all statements that the front end might wish
  to run "behind the scenes" in order to give a richer view of the
  state of Magma.  So care should be taken in such cases to handle
  this correctly.

* SIG is an output tag.  It is used when printing signatures.

  Syntax:

	SIG<space><indent-level><marker><text>
	SIG<space><continuation-flag><marker><text>

  Meanings are as described for output tags above.

  Behaviour:

  The given text should be shown to the user.  If a richer display
  is wished, the front end will need to parse this format in order
  to get the divisions.  Fortunately this is relatively easy, with
  the indent values letting us distinguish the components:

  + indent = 0 is used for "header" information like the intrinsic
    or "Signatures relevant to <type>" messages.
  + indent = 1 is used for the signature proper.
  + indent = 2 is used for the description of a signature.

  When running in -x mode, magma always provides a description for
  each signature, unlike normal mode which may collapse signatures
  with the same description into a block of multiple signatures to
  the one description.  This simplifies any such parsing.

  There is one potential "gotcha": If IndentPush() has been called
  then the indent values may be greater than indicated above.  The
  more robust solution is to determine the base indent level using
  the first SIG line, and then check for indent levels of base + 1
  and base + 2.

  Semantics:

  SIG indicates that the output is a list of signatures.  This may
  be due to an intrinsic being printed or a call to ListSignatures
  in one of its forms.  Any header information (like the intrinsic
  name or "Signatures relevant to <type>") will also use this tag.


* INT is a status tag.  It arises when Magma receives an interrupt
  signal and stops the current computation.

  Syntax:

	INT

  Behaviour:

  The user should be notified that computation was interrupted, if
  one was running.  If no computation was running then this can be
  ignored.  The two cases can be distinguished by seeing which tag
  of RDY or RUN was most recent prior to the interrupt.  If it was
  RUN then a computation was interrupted; otherwise no computation
  was interrupted.

  The front end is expected to provide some convenient way for the
  user to perform this interrupt themselves.  In terminal versions
  responding to ctrl-C is the most obvious way, but more graphical
  front ends might provide a button instead and then send a signal
  to the underlying Magma process.


* RES is a status tag, indicating that a complete reset of Magma's
  state.  This could be due to either use of the "clear" directive
  or restoring a workspace.  In each case, information gathered by
  the front end may need to be deleted and re-read.

  Syntax:

	RES

  Behaviour:

  If the front end tracks things like the current list of assigned
  identifiers or similar, then these must be considered invalid if
  a RES is received.  Otherwise this can be ignored.

  Note that a RDY following a RES will not indicate changes in any
  of the fields it tracks even if the resetting action causes such
  changes.  The RES is considered sufficient notification of this.


* ENE is a status tag, indicating that the final part of the input
  set did not form a valid Magma statement.

  Syntax:

	ENE

  Behaviour:

  A message should be displayed to the user stating that the input
  set could not be fully parsed.  An attempt could be made to show
  the specific text involved, but this is not required.  (Starting
  from the entire input set, any RUN lines can be used to rule out
  correctly-handled parts.  Then one might wish to skip whitespace
  and even comments, if feeling industrious, until settling on the
  probable start of the bad statement.)


* DRDY is a status tag, which indicates that the debugger has been
  invoked.  It can only happen if SetDebugOnError(true) was called
  earlier and an error has arisen.  The front end may have earlier
  called SetDebugOnError(true) itself, for that matter, if it aims
  to provide debug access on demand.

  Syntax:

	DRDY

  Behaviour:

  If the front end presents a continuous view of the session, like
  a terminal version does, then it should engage with the debugger 
  at this point.  If it takes a more discontinuous view, such as a
  Jupyter notebook would, then it should note that the debugger is
  currently enabled.  If the user opts to use the debugger when it
  is enabled then the front end should engage with it.  Otherwise,
  if the user executes more code, then the front end must exit the
  debugger (by sending it the debugger "q" command).

  "Engaging" with the debugger entails entering a more interactive
  mode where user input is processed one line at a time and passed
  onto the debugger, then any response is handled.  In addition to
  any output that might arise from the command, either a DRDY or a
  RDY will be returned: DRDY if the debugger is still running, and
  RDY if the debugger has exited and the regular magma interpreter 
  is listening.

  Important: Debugger input is handled on a line-by-line basis, so
  the input must not be followed by an end-of-input marker in this
  case.  The standard newline suffices.

  If a prompt is printed, then it should be visibly different from
  the usual magma prompt.  The prompt that Magma uses is "debug> "
  which is a perfectly serviceable choice.


* DTB is an output tag, indicating a traceback in the debugger.

  Syntax:

	DTB<space><indent-level><marker><text>
	DTB<space><continuation-flag><marker><text>

  Meanings are as described for output tags above.

  Behaviour:

  The given text should be shown to the user.  The front end might
  choose to parse it in order to present a nice view; in that case
  I suggest looking at a debug traceback to infer the format -- it
  seems fairly simple, but I have not actually checked.

  Semantics:

  DTB indicates that this output is part of a traceback printed by
  the Magma debugger.  This will only occur if the user has called
  the "bt" / "backtrace" debugger command while in the debugger.


* DE is an output tag, giving an error message from the debugger.

  Syntax:

	DE<space><indent-level><marker><text>
	DE<space><continuation-flag><marker><text>

  Meanings are as described for output tags above.

  Behaviour:

  The given text should be shown to the user.  Since this is known
  to be an error message, the front end may display it in whatever
  it regards as an appropriate manner for errors.

  Semantics:

  The given text is an error message from the debugger.  It can be
  triggered in two circumstances: The user uses the "fr" / "frame"
  directive with an invalid frame number, or the user asks to list
  a non-positive number of lines with the "l" / "list" directive.


* RD_PR is an output tag, giving a prompt to show to the user when
  using the "read" directive.

  Syntax:

	RD_PR<space><indent-level><marker><text>
	RD_PR<space><continuation-flag><marker><text>

  Meanings are as described for output tags above.

  Behaviour:

  The given text (if any) should be shown to the user.  It will be
  the prompt string provided with a "read" directive.  If there is
  a separate location provided for input requests (such as a popup
  window or special input area) then this prompt should be printed
  there.

  Semantics:

  The given text is a prompt for a "read" directive; there will be
  a request for a line of input next (using an RD_IN tag).


* RD_IN is a status tag, indicating that a line of input is wanted
  from the user in response to a "read" directive.

  Syntax:

	RD_IN

  Behaviour:

  A line of input must be retrieved from the user and then written
  to the magma back end.  The front end might choose to open a new
  window for this input, or activate a special input area for it.

  Important: Precisely one line of input is wanted, and we are not
  constructing an input set.  So this line must not be followed by
  an end-of-input marker; the standard newline suffices.

  Note: If an interrupt signal is sent to magma then the read will
  be interrupted.  Whatever means the front end uses to allow such
  signals must also abort this read when used.


* RDI_PR is an output tag, containing a prompt to show to the user
  when using the "readi" directive.

  Syntax:

	RDI_PR<space><indent-level><marker><text>
	RDI_PR<space><continuation-flag><marker><text>

  Meanings are as described for output tags above.

  Behaviour:

  The given text (if any) should be shown to the user.  It will be
  the prompt string provided with the "readi" directive.  If there
  is a separate location provided for input requests (like a popup
  window or special input area) then this prompt should be printed
  there.

  Semantics:

  The given text is the prompt for a "readi" directive; there will
  be a request for a line of input next (using an RDI_IN tag).


* RDI_IN is a status tag, which indicates that an integer input is
  wanted from the user in response to a "readi" directive.

  Syntax:

	RDI_IN

  Behaviour:

  A line of input must be retrieved from the user and then written
  to the magma back end.  The front end might choose to open a new
  window for this input, or activate a special input area for it.

  Important: Precisely one line of input is wanted, and we are not
  constructing an input set.  So this line must not be followed by
  an end-of-input marker; the standard newline suffices.

  Note: If an interrupt signal is sent to magma then the read will
  be interrupted.  Whatever means the front end uses to allow such
  signals must also abort this read when used.


* RDI_ER is an output tag, indicating that there was an error with
  the input that the user supplied to a previous "readi".

  Syntax:

	RDI_ER<space><indent-level><marker><text>
	RDI_ER<space><continuation-flag><marker><text>

  Behaviour:

  The given text should be shown to the user.  Since this is known
  to be an error message, the front end may display it in whatever
  it regards as an appropriate manner for errors.

  The error does not terminate the read request, which still needs
  to be satisfied.  Consequently, after showing the error message,
  this tag can be treated as though it were RDI_IN.

  Semantics:

  RDI_ER indicates an error in the user's input, as an integer was
  required but presumably they did not give one.


Tags (the messy details)
------------------------

Here we describe the meaning of the fields provided with data tags
and how they might be used.

* RDY has five arguments, each of which indicates whether a change
  has occurred to some user-visible state of the interpreter.  The
  idea behind this is that the front end might automatically track
  these changes for the user (if asked to do so), by sending extra
  queries to Magma behind the scenes.

  The meanings of these five fields (in order) are:

  + Whether the list of assigned identifiers has changed since the
    last RDY line (or since startup, if there was no such previous
    line).

    This field is either 0 (meaning no change) or 1 (meaning there
    was a change).

    The front end might display the currently assigned identifiers
    to the user in some fashion.  If so, then when this field is 1
    the front end could make a call to ShowIdentifiers() to update
    this list.

    Note that a change could either mean that a new identifier has
    been assigned to, or that a previously assigned identifier has
    been deleted, or both.  Also note that it is possible there is
    no net change in the identifier list even if this flag is set;
    for example, a new identifier might have been created and then
    deleted within the same input set.

  + Whether the value of some identifier has changed, including if
    a new identifier has been assigned to, since the last RDY line
    (or since startup, if there was no such previous line).

    This field is either 0 (meaning no change) or 1 (meaning there
    was a change).

    The front end might display the values of assigned identifiers
    to the user in some fashion.  If so, then when this field is 1
    the front end could print each identifier to get the new value
    for display.  The user might perhaps specify which identifiers
    they are interested in monitoring this way, in order to reduce
    the amount of extra printing done.

  + Whether some verbose value has been changed since the last RDY
    line (or since startup, if there was no such previous line).

    This field is either 0 (meaning no change) or 1 (meaning there
    was a change).

    The front end can display the possible verbose flags and their
    current values, possibly providing a more convenient means for
    interacting with them.  The verbose flags and their values can 
    be retrieved by calling DumpVerbose (this also gives the limit
    for each verbose flag) at startup, and each time that a change
    is indicated by this field.

    The front end might also enable the user to modify the verbose
    flags from whatever interface it provides, and call SetVerbose
    on the user's behalf to do so.

  + Whether some global configuration value has been changed using
    one of the various Set* intrinsics.

    This field is either 0 (meaning no change) or 1 (meaning there
    was a change).

    The front end might again provide an alternative interface for
    these things, perhaps from a carefully chosen subset of values
    that are deemed useful.  If this field indicates a change then
    the front end would have to query all values of interest using
    the corresponding Get* intrinsics to find the changes.

    The list of Set* intrinsics which trigger this behaviour are:

    - SetAssertions
    - SetAutoColumns
    - SetAutoCompact
    - SetBeep
    - SetColumns
    - SetDefaultLieType
    - SetDefaultRealField
    - SetDefaultRealFieldPrecision
    - SetEchoInput
    - SetHistorySize
    - SetIgnorePrompt
    - SetIgnoreSpaces
    - SetIndent
    - SetLibraries
    - SetLibraryRoot
    - SetLineEditor
    - SetMemoryExtensionSize
    - SetMemoryLimit
    - SetPath
    - SetPreviousSize
    - SetPrintClassGroupWarnings
    - SetPrintLevel
    - SetPrompt
    - SetQuitOnDoubleInterrupt
    - SetQuitOnError
    - SetRows
    - SetSeed
    - SetShellCompletion
    - SetShowPromptAlways
    - SetTraceback
    - SetUseMontes
    - SetVerbose
    - SetViMode

    Plus some others that don't have corresponding Get* intrinsics
    or are intended for internal use only.

    As mentioned earlier, changing the number of spaces per indent
    with SetIndent will cause this field to be set.  However, this
    is of limited use since printing with the changed indent value
    may already have occurred.  That said, the front end is likely
    to have a better way of displaying indentation and should feel
    free to ignore the specific number of spaces set by the user.

  + The difference in the number of types between now and the last
    RDY line (or since startup, if there was no such line).

    This field is either 0 or positive.  It cannot be negative.

    If this field is positive then that many types have been added
    to the system in the interim, presumably due to calling Attach
    or AttachSpec.  The front end could insert a call to ListTypes
    in order to get the new list of valid type names.


  In addition to the various possibilities suggested for the front
  end behaviour above, being able to track these values might help
  the front end do completion or syntax highlighting.


* RUN has six arguments.  The first two are the random number seed
  information prior to executing this statement, as would be given
  by calling GetSeed.  Both numbers are non-negative; the first is
  less than 2^32 and the second is less than 2^64.  i.e., they are
  unsigned 32-bit and 64-bit numbers, respectively.

  The other four arguments specify positions within the input set;
  the first two identify the start of the statement while the last
  two identify the end of the statement.  A position consists of a
  line number and a column number; both are 0-based.  For example,
  the position 0 0 means the first character in the first line and
  the position 2 10 would mean the eleventh character in the third
  line.

  A specific example may help here; the '?' characters are used in
  place of the tag marker (value 129) for clarity.

	$ magma -xbn
	?RDY 0 0 0 0 667
	> 1 + 1 -
	> 3*2 + 7^2;
	> u := Random(4);
	> print "hello";
	> 
	IR
	?RUN 1 0 0 0 1 9
	?OUT 0?45
	?RUN 1 0 2 0 2 14
	?RUN 1 1 3 0 3 13
	?OUT 0?hello
	?RDY 1 1 0 0 0

  The first RDY line tells us there are 667 types at start.  After
  the IR, we start running the first input statement.  This is the
  statement corresponding to this text:

	> 1 + 1 -
	> 3*2 + 7^2;

  The first RUN line is

	?RUN 1 0 0 0 1 9

  which tells us the following:

  * The random state prior to running this statement was (1, 0).
  * The statement starts at (0, 0) in the input set, which is line
    1 column 1.
  * The statement ends at (1, 9) in the input set, which is line 2
    column 10 -- the location of the semicolon.

  (Keep in mind that "> " is a prompt displayed to the user and is
  not part of the input set.)

  We get the output "45" from this line.  The next statement to be
  executed is

	> u := Random(4);

  , with corresponding RUN line

	?RUN 1 0 2 0 2 14

  Here we see that the random state is the same as for the earlier
  statement.  This statement starts at (2, 0), the first character
  of the third line, then ends at (2, 14), the fifteenth character
  of the third line.

  There is no output from that line.  The next statement is this:

	> print "hello";

  The associated RUN line is

	?RUN 1 1 3 0 3 13

  We see that the random state has advanced by one step due to the
  call to Random in the previous statement and is now (1, 1).  The
  positions of the start and end of the statement are as expected.

  Note the final RDY line:

	?RDY 1 1 0 0 0

  We see that the assignment to "u" in the third line has produced
  a change in the identifier list as well as a change in the value
  of an identifier.


* ERP has four arguments.  These are two positions relative to the
  current input set.  As with RUN, each position is made up of two
  integers, with the first specifying a line number and the second
  specifying a column position within that line.  Both are 0-based
  so (for instance) the position 2 7 means the eighth character of
  the third line.

  The first position is the start of the statement that caused the
  error, while the second position is where parsing stopped due to
  having detected the error.

  ERP indicates an error while parsing, which we call "user error"
  in normal Magma use.

  Example (as usual, ? indicates a marker byte with value 129):

	> 1 + 1; 3*2 - 1/0 - 7;
	> 
	?IR
	?RUN 1 0 0 0 0 5
	?OUT 0?2
	?ERP 0 7 0 17
	?POS 0 15
	?EU 0?User error: Illegal zero denominator
	?RDY 0 0 0 0 0

  The four integers in the ERP entry produce the positions 0 7 and
  0 17.  These correspond to the '3' and the '-' between '1/0' and
  '7'.  So we see where the bad statement started and how far into
  it the parser had got before finding an error.

  Note that we then also have a POS line giving the exact location
  of the error, and an EU line giving the error text.


* POS has two arguments, which specify a position within the input
  set.  As usual, the first argument specifies a line number while
  the second specifies a column number.  Both are 0-based, so (for
  example) the position 5 2 means the third character of the sixth
  line.

  The position gives the location of the parse error, which is the
  first character of the token that caused the parsing to fail.

  In the example for ERP above, the specified position corresponds
  to the '0' in '1/0'.  At this point the parser tried to make the
  literal rational number 1/0, which failed, of course.  Note that
  the second position in the ERP entry is after that point.  While
  the 0 was the underlying cause of the problem, the parser had to
  look a little further ahead to determine that.

